# The bytes of every binary that decides whether a signature is good.
#
# tiverify is what checks the Ed25519 signature on a plan for Linux and
# macOS; tisig.dll does the same job on Windows, and also walks the
# Merkle proof. Until 2026-09-24 every hash recorded for the first five
# was wrong -- the binaries were rebuilt and the table was not -- and the
# sixth had no recorded hash anywhere in the tree at all. The engines'
# known-answer test catches a broken verifier, not a targeted one: a
# verifier that also accepts one extra key answers RFC 8032 correctly.
#
# So this file is the record, it is in git, and the build scripts check
# against it. Rebuilding a binary means changing this file in a commit,
# which is the point -- the same reason plan-key.id exists.
#
# Check by hand: sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS (from the repository root).
